course: dictionary
lesson: 3759
title: Compound words
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A compound word is made by joining two smaller words. Flyover is made of fly and over. When you split a word into its parts, its meaning often becomes clearer.
explain: Flyover has more than one meaning, so check the sentence around it. In the UK and Australia, a flyover is a bridge that carries one road over another road or a railway. In the USA, a flyover is a planned flight of aircraft low over a place for a show or ceremony.
explain: To check your idea, put it back into the sentence. If you read «We drove under the flyover», does a bridge fit? It does, so the first meaning is the right one here.
fact: A compound word joins two words, like butter + fly = butterfly.
fact: A chain of compound words can go flyover, overtime, timetable.
fact: «Flyover» means a bridge that carries one road over another road or railway (UK, Australia).
fact: In the USA, a flyover, also called a flypast in the UK, is a planned flight of aircraft low over a place for a show or ceremony.
fact: «Flyover country» is a joking US name for the middle states that people fly over between the coasts.
exercise: Which two words are joined to make flyover? | flow and over | fly and high | *fly and over | sky and over
exercise: In the UK and Australia, what is a flyover? | *A bridge that carries one road over another road or a railway | A tunnel under a river | A small plane for hire | A shop at the airport
exercise: In the USA, what can a flyover be at a ceremony? | A bridge over a railway | A sudden storm | A famous old castle | *A planned flight of aircraft low over a place
test: Which word joins butter and fly? | *Butterfly | Buttery | Flyer
test: Flyover country is a joke name for what? | A busy road in London | The bridges of Scotland | Places where pilots eat lunch | *The middle states of the USA that people fly over between the coasts
exam: Mum says, «Meet me under the flyover», and you wait by a road under a bridge. What does flyover mean here? | A flight of aircraft low over a show | *A bridge that carries one road over another road or a railway | A kind of fly that lives in the air | A long line of traffic jams
